A||||5||PAUL88GT||Archie's Clutch Pedal Replacement Procedure||||1||
Z||000000||PAUL88GT||03-15-2001||11:12 AM||||I just used Archie's clutch pedel replacement procedure for my 86SE. I have to say that the procedure is excellent.  Even though my old aluminum pedal was only slightly bent, the new steel one solved my clutch disengagement problem. It doesn't take much pedal bending to cause a problem on the total throw of the slave cylinder rod.<P>I would recommend a couple of additions to the procedure:<P>1) You have to remove a relay or small silver electrical box before the primary shaft/bolt can be retracted in order to remove the clutch pedal assembly.<BR>2) The new steel pedal does not have the bushing needed to remove the play from the the master cylinder banjo fitting and the small pin on the clutch pedal. The newer master cylinder has a plastic bushing in the banjo fitting, the original ones do not. I acquired a plastic bushing from a bad master cylinder at the Fiero Factory. I could not locate a metal bushing anywhere in our town including speed shops.  I looked for several hours without success. Without a bushing, you would need to replace both the clutch pedal and the OEM master cylinder at the same time.<P>Other than the comments above, Archie's procedure is dead-on.<P>This was a very difficult procedure since you are working upside down and the space to move you arms is very small.  Also, the light is very near your face.  It is doable even for an amateur mechanic.<BR>Paul||||reg||1
Z||000001||Archie||03-15-2001||12:03 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by PAUL88GT:<BR><B> I could not locate a metal bushing anywhere in our town including speed shops.  <P>Other than the comments above, Archie's procedure is dead-on.<P>Paul</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Next time you are at the speed shop or Pep Boys, ask for a replacement Bushing kit for a Hurst Chevy shifter. They run about $10 and have like 8 bushings that work perfect on this installation.<P>Archie||||reg||1
Z||000002||PAUL88GT||03-15-2001||05:20 PM||||Thanks Archie for the information on the bushings. We don't have PEP Boys. I tried Autozone, Advanced Auto, NAPA, Pontiac Dealer, Chevy Dealer, and the local speed shop with no success. I was just about to go have a bushing machined when I decided to see Ed at the Fiero Factory and came up with this other solution.<P>My hope is that I don't have to change another clutch pedal.<P>Paul<p>[This message has been edited by PAUL88GT (edited 03-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Ronnie||03-15-2001||10:44 PM||||Got the solution to the bushing problem.A fellow member of our Fiero club gave me a brass ferrule, like you use on gas line tubing, works perfect and won't wear out as fast as plastic. Fixed my clutch problem along with the steel clutch pedal of course.You can buy those ferrules at Lowes or Home Depot for pennies!<P>------------------<BR>Black '86GT 4spd ,leather int.<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fiero2_1_3.jpg"> ||||reg||6
Z||000004||FieroJoe||03-16-2001||10:46 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>You have to remove a relay or small silver electrical box<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That is the turn signal flasher relay, there is a similar one next to the chime box on the other side for the hazard lights.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>fellow member of our Fiero club gave me a brass ferrule,like you use on gas line tubing<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Those are the compression rings used on water and air fittings.  Can be used on gas, but normally flared fittings are used on gas.  Ace Hardware also carries them, if you dont want to spend 5 hours looking for them at one of those big places.<P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Phil||03-16-2001||10:53 AM||||For a bushing I went to my local ACE hardware store and found an electric motor bearing. Think it cost about $0.75||||reg||1
